If Parquet is the ultimate library optimized for reading data, Avro is the ultimate printing press optimized for writing it.
The choice between them isn't a battle of which format is objectively superior; it is entirely dictated by whether your system is read-heavy or write-heavy.

The architectural divide comes down to how they lay out data on disk:

Parquet (Columnar): It stores data by columns. This allows query engines to use 'Predicate Pushdown' to skip irrelevant columns and files entirely,
making it phenomenally fast and heavily compressed for analytical (OLAP) queries like GROUP BY or SUM.

Avro (Row-Based): It stores data row-by-row in a compact binary format. Because it doesn't have to transpose incoming records into columns, appending new data is incredibly fast.
Crucially, Avro embeds its own schema—written in plain JSON—directly inside the binary file header. 
This makes it the undisputed king of robust schema evolution, as the file explicitly tells the downstream engine exactly how to deserialize the payload,
even if fields have been added, renamed, or deleted.

Buisness Context :- 
Using the wrong format destroys infrastructure budgets. If you store your massive analytical data lake entirely in Avro, your BI dashboards will be forced to execute 
full-table scans for every query, sending your cloud I/O bills through the roof. Conversely, if you force a high-throughput, real-time data stream to write directly into columnar Parquet,
the CPU overhead required to constantly transpose individual rows into columns will bottleneck your entire ingestion architecture.

Design Decision :- 
Some engineers often try to standardize on a single file format for the entire company to simplify the codebase.
As a data engineer, I architect systems that leverage both. I strictly mandate Avro for the ingestion layer—specifically as the 
payload format for Apache Kafka event streams—because of its seamless integration with the Confluent Schema Registry and lightning-fast write speeds.
But the moment that streaming data lands in our 'Bronze' Data Lake, my Spark jobs instantly rewrite it into Parquet for the 'Silver' and 'Gold' layers, optimizing it for cheap,
lightning-fast analytical reads by the data science team.
